No arguments there. What's unclear is how you manage the AC issues. 
Wizards with a naked AC of 10 are sitting ducks. And with the armor
restrictions with virtually every piece of armor dropped (other than
leather), AC acquistion is a critical need. With the MPR strategy
you wear whatever armor is available long enough to complete the run.
Then with the low naked AC the character is better equipped to handle
monsters that the dungeon will generate.

I believe that's the problem the OP was having. They would level up 
then get crushed by what the dungeon generated. But they needed to
level up in order to compete with the dungeon. It's an arms race and
IMHO an unfair one. To balance it out you need AC. And the MPR provides
that.

But you can't do the MPR stupidly. Diving into the mines at AC9 with an XL1
pet and XL1 yourself is a death sentence. I think folks try that, it
doesn't work, then call the MPR silly. In the MPR you have to unbalance the
game in several ways:

1) Instead of leveling up normally, you level up controlled. You abnormally
level up your pet, which has no game consequnce being XL9 with 72HP. Let
the pet be your tank.

2) You gain HP abnormally by praying.

3) You take the armor restiction hit temporarily to get much better AC.
No normal wizard can wear dwarvish mithril. But one on the MPR can because
it'll be ditched at the end of the run.

4) Instead of exploring deeper into the dungeon, which generates tougher
monsters, you explore shallowly. The dungeon helps be generating early
shops and shops in MineTown. All of this is shallow.

In short you unbalance the game in for favor for a while to obtain the
reward of unbalancing the game in your favor permanently with the
protection AC. And unlike praying for protection, it's guaranteed to be
doable in the early game.

